<ArticleLayout slug="raw-material-processing-cereals">
  <p>
    Processing cereals and protein crops by extrusion cooking offers definite
    advantages:
  </p>

  <h2>Starch gelatinisation</h2>
  <p>
    It improves the digestibility of the carbohydrates in the raw materials.
    Using a preconditioner with the addition of water and steam makes this
    gelatinisation easier and considerably increases the output of the
    extruder. Extrusion processing improves the efficiency of carbohydrates
    in young animals, or in species where amylase production is poorly
    developed.
  </p>

  <h2>Reduced protein solubility</h2>
  <p>
    The very short time during which the product to be extruded is subjected
    to very high temperatures does not cause any drop in protein
    digestibility due to the Maillard reaction.
  </p>
  <p>
    Extrusion causes the insolubilisation of the native proteins contained in
    protein crops and oilseeds. This transformation is particularly valuable
    for the use of these raw materials in ruminant feed, since it lowers the
    proportion of soluble nitrogen in these ingredients (PDIN) and increases
    the content of nitrogen that is not degradable in the rumen (PDIA).
  </p>

  <h2>The other transformations</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      the destruction of antinutritional factors, including 90% of the
      antitrypsic factors contained in soybean and protein crops,
    </li>
    <li>
      the reduction of the glucosinolate content of rapeseed and rapeseed
      meal, of gossypol in cotton and of aflatoxins in groundnut meal and in
      ingredients contaminated by these moulds,
    </li>
    <li>
      the inactivation of the enzyme systems responsible for the release of
      hydrocyanic acid from linseed and linseed meal, as well as of the
      lipases and lipoxidases present in oilseeds - preventing the rancidity
      and oxidation of the oil they contain,
    </li>
    <li>
      the release of the fat contained in the cells: increased metabolisable
      energy, stabilisation of the fat and reduced oxidation of the oil,
    </li>
    <li>
      the forming of the product on exit: passing through dies at the outlet
      of the extruder gives products of widely varied sizes and shapes.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <a
      href="https://maurice.legoy.free.fr/extrusion/transformations/index.htm"
      hreflang="fr"
